@model Edulytics.Core.Curriculum.LessonContent.RichLessonV2

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U05:L01")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u05-l01 aria-label="Grade 7 signed rational interpretation">
    <h3>Signed Numbers in Context — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>1.1 Place values relative to zero</h4>
    <p>Interpret positive and negative values as positions relative to a reference such as sea level or zero degrees. Plot fractional and decimal values on horizontal and vertical number lines.</p>
    <h4>1.2 Compare rational values</h4>
    <p>Order signed fractions and decimals by their actual positions, not by the size of the digits. For negative values, farther left or lower means smaller.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4>
    <p>A diver is at -7.5 m, a sensor is at -13/2 m, and a bird is at 2.25 m relative to sea level. Order the three elevations from least to greatest, identify which two are closest, and sketch their locations on a vertical number line.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check number-line placement, contextual meaning, and comparison justification. Machine Practice checks exact rational comparison only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U05:L02")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u05-l02 aria-label="Grade 7 changing temperatures">
    <h3>Changing Temperatures — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>2.1 Start, change, finish</h4><p>Represent a temperature change as starting value plus signed change. A rise is positive; a fall is negative.</p>
    <h4>2.2 Draw the motion</h4><p>On a number line, begin at the starting temperature and move right/up for a positive change or left/down for a negative change.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>The temperature is -6.5 C and then rises 9.25 C. Find the final temperature. Later it falls 4.75 C. Find the new temperature and write both changes as signed addition expressions.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check number-line direction, story-to-expression translation and units. Machine Practice checks exact signed sums only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U05:L03")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u05-l03 aria-label="Grade 7 changing elevation">
    <h3>Changing Elevation — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>3.1 Combine direction and magnitude</h4><p>When signs match, combine magnitudes and keep the sign. When signs differ, compare magnitudes and keep the direction of the larger magnitude.</p>
    <h4>3.2 Check with a number line</h4><p>Interpret the first number as a starting elevation and the second as a signed change.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A cave entrance is at 18.5 m and a chamber is reached after a change of -27.75 m. Find the chamber elevation. Then a passage rises 6.25 m; find the new elevation and explain each sign.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check elevation interpretation and generalization of opposite-sign addition. Machine Practice checks exact signed rational sums/comparisons.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U05:L04")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u05-l04 aria-label="Grade 7 money and debts">
    <h3>Money, Credits, and Debts — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>4.1 Give the sign a meaning</h4><p>Use positive values for money owned or added and negative values for debt, charges, or withdrawals, according to the stated account convention.</p>
    <h4>4.2 Reconcile a balance</h4><p>Add the signed transactions in order and explain which events increase or decrease the balance.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>An account begins at -12.50. A 30.00 deposit is made, followed by a 7.75 charge and a 4.25 refund. Find the final balance and classify each transaction as a positive or negative change.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check financial meaning of signs and transaction interpretation. Machine Practice checks exact signed sums only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U05:L05")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u05-l05 aria-label="Grade 7 representing subtraction">
    <h3>Subtract by Adding the Opposite — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>5.1 Rewrite before calculating</h4><p>For any rational numbers a and b, a-b and a+(-b) have the same value.</p>
    <h4>5.2 Interpret on a number line</h4><p>Subtracting a positive moves left; subtracting a negative has the same effect as adding its positive opposite.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>Rewrite and evaluate -3.5-(-2.25) and 1.75-4.5 as addition expressions. Explain the direction of each movement on a number line.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check the equivalence argument and number-line movement. Machine Practice checks exact signed differences only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U05:L06")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u05-l06 aria-label="Grade 7 rational subtraction and distance">
    <h3>Difference and Distance — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>6.1 Signed difference depends on order</h4><p>a-b tells the directed change from b to a; reversing the subtraction changes the sign.</p>
    <h4>6.2 Distance is absolute</h4><p>The distance between two positions is |a-b| and is never negative, regardless of which point is named first.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>One point is at -14.5 m and another at 6.75 m. Find both signed differences and the distance between them. Explain why only the distance is order-independent.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check the distinction between directed difference and physical distance. Machine Practice checks exact difference and distance subresults.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U05:L07")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u05-l07 aria-label="Grade 7 signed problem solving">
    <h3>Signed Gains, Losses, and Net Change — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>7.1 Translate each event</h4><p>Assign a sign to each gain or loss, then combine changes in the order required by the context.</p>
    <h4>7.2 Net change is not total movement</h4><p>Net change compares final and initial values; total movement or distance may be larger because opposite movements do not cancel physically.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A café starts with 120 reusable cups, sends out 47, receives 18 back, sends out 36, and receives 29. Find the final inventory and the net change from the start.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check event signs and interpretation of net change. Machine Practice checks exact signed arithmetic only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U05:L08")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u05-l08 aria-label="Grade 7 position speed and direction">
    <h3>Position, Speed, and Direction — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>8.1 Choose a positive direction</h4><p>Declare which direction is positive before assigning signs to positions or velocities.</p>
    <h4>8.2 Signed velocity produces signed displacement</h4><p>Displacement equals velocity times elapsed time; the sign records direction while magnitude records distance per unit time.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>East is positive. A cart moves west at 2.5 m/s for 8 seconds from position 6 m. Find its signed displacement and final position. Explain both signs.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check the chosen orientation and distinction between speed, velocity, displacement, and position. Machine Practice checks exact signed products/displacement.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U05:L09")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u05-l09 aria-label="Grade 7 multiplying rational numbers">
    <h3>Multiply Signed Rational Numbers — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>9.1 Separate sign from magnitude</h4><p>Multiply magnitudes, then use sign structure: same signs give a positive product and different signs give a negative product.</p>
    <h4>9.2 Interpret negative time in a model carefully</h4><p>When a model uses time before a reference moment, a negative time can combine with a negative rate to describe an earlier positive value relative to the reference.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>Evaluate (-3/4)(-10/9), (-1.2)(5), and a temperature model with rate -2.5 C/hour at t=-3 hours relative to a zero reference.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check sign-rule explanation and interpretation of model variables. Machine Practice checks exact signed products.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U05:L10")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u05-l10 aria-label="Grade 7 signed multiplication fluency">
    <h3>Multiply with Sign and Structure — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>10.1 Predict the sign before multiplying</h4><p>Use the number of negative factors to predict the sign, then multiply and simplify magnitudes.</p>
    <h4>10.2 Diagnose an incorrect product</h4><p>Separate errors in the sign from errors in multiplying or simplifying the numerical factors.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A student claims (-5/6)(-9/10)=-3/4. Decide whether the claim is correct, give the correct product, and identify whether the student's likely mistake is sign-related or magnitude-related.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check error diagnosis and sign reasoning. Machine Practice checks exact signed products only.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U05:L11")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u05-l11 aria-label="Grade 7 dividing rational numbers">
    <h3>Divide Signed Rational Numbers — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>11.1 Predict the quotient sign</h4><p>Same signs give a positive quotient; different signs give a negative quotient.</p>
    <h4>11.2 Multiply by the reciprocal</h4><p>Replace division by a nonzero rational number with multiplication by its reciprocal, then simplify exactly.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>Evaluate (-7/8) divided by (14/3), and (2.4) divided by (-0.6). For each, state the expected sign before calculating and verify by multiplication.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check reciprocal reasoning and verification. Machine Practice checks exact signed quotients.</p>
  </section>
}

@if (Model.LessonCode == "PED:US-CCSS-MATH:G7:U05:L12")
{
  <section class="rich-lesson-section" data-g7-u05-l12 aria-label="Grade 7 negative rates">
    <h3>Negative Rates — Edulytiks</h3>
    <h4>12.1 A negative rate describes direction of change</h4><p>A rate below zero means the modeled quantity decreases relative to the chosen positive direction.</p>
    <h4>12.2 Solve for time or change</h4><p>Use change=rate×time or time=change/rate, preserving signs and interpreting whether the resulting time makes sense in context.</p>
    <h4>Cool-down — first-party Edulytiks assessment</h4><p>A submarine changes depth at -18 m/min for 7.5 minutes from -40 m. Find the signed displacement and final elevation. Then find how long a -90 m displacement would take at the same rate.</p>
    <p><strong>Teacher review:</strong> Check rate sign, reference direction and physical interpretation. Machine Practice checks exact signed rate arithmetic.</p>
  </section>
}
